And then of course, the same thing where it happens to be that the entrant has lower costs, then the entrant just puts the incumbent out of business.

203
00:17:48,840 --> 00:17:50,840
Yes.

204
00:17:50,840 --> 00:17:54,840
If we choose to increase its price, its quantity is still down, right?

205
00:17:54,840 --> 00:17:59,840
Yeah. We still have a regular demand curve. We still have a regular demand curve.

206
00:17:59,840 --> 00:18:03,840
So yeah. So when you go and raise price, it reduces quantity.

207
00:18:03,840 --> 00:18:12,840
Yes. So it's true that you also need to make sure you aren't charging a price so high that even a regular monopolist wouldn't want to charge that high.

208
00:18:12,840 --> 00:18:23,840
So in other words, implicit in all these models, is that the actual solution is better than the traditional one for monopolists over here?

209
00:18:23,840 --> 00:18:33,840
So, I guess I got rid of it.

210
00:18:33,840 --> 00:18:38,840
I guess it was over here. Yes. So we're here. So we're raising marginal revenue equals marginal cost.

211
00:18:38,840 --> 00:18:45,840
So strictly speaking, you want to charge the higher of the intersection or that.

212
00:18:45,840 --> 00:18:47,840
Yes. Yeah.

213
00:18:47,840 --> 00:18:54,840
So, I mean, think about this. There could be one monopolist in the world, one firm produces cars, and then the potential entrant is me.

214
00:18:54,840 --> 00:18:58,840
And I don't know anything about where I would start producing cars.

215
00:18:58,840 --> 00:19:08,840
So the fact that I'm a potential entrant with costs that are extraordinarily high does not going to have any constraining effect on the car monopolist's behavior.

216
00:19:08,840 --> 00:19:15,840
Instead, he'll just act like a regular monopolist because the potential entrant wouldn't enter at the monopoly price.

217
00:19:15,840 --> 00:19:19,840
I couldn't be profitable at the monopoly price for General Motors.

218
00:19:19,840 --> 00:19:24,840
I probably couldn't be profitable at ten times that price. I just wouldn't have any clue of even where to start.

219
00:19:24,840 --> 00:19:26,840
I guess I'd call my dad.

220
00:19:26,840 --> 00:19:31,840
Dad, how do I start a car firm?

221
00:19:31,840 --> 00:19:34,840
You're a car guy, right? You know car people.

222
00:19:34,840 --> 00:19:37,840
All right, so let's make cars.

223
00:19:37,840 --> 00:19:40,840
All right, so that wouldn't work too well.

224
00:19:40,840 --> 00:19:43,840
That is right, sir.

225
00:19:43,840 --> 00:19:51,840
All right, now, so far we've been doing fixed costs, but then there's the question, what if it were a subcost?

